---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
---

<Docs
  title="CI integration"
  description="CI health workflow generation is unavailable. Inspect the refusal and optionally collect a separate local source-drift report."
  eyebrow="Integrations"
>
  <h2>The shape</h2>
  <p>
    In the reviewed source, CI health workflow generation is unavailable:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      Review the <strong><a href="https://phm.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-[color:var(--color-ink-100)] underline decoration-dotted decoration-[#3b82f6]/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-[#3b82f6] hover:text-[#3b82f6] transition-colors">Phantom</a> Cloud</strong> boundary: unattended pull is unsupported by Phantom Secrets 0.7.9.
    </li>
    <li>
      Inspect <code>stack ci init</code>. The admitted source refuses workflow generation with exit code <code>1</code>; it does not write a workflow.
    </li>
    <li>
      Optionally collect a JSON source-drift report separately. It is not a credentialed health check or working CI bootstrap.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Unattended Cloud pull is unsupported with Phantom Secrets 0.7.9. Cloud pull requires stdin, stdout and stderr on a trusted terminal; --force only declares overwrites and does not bypass that requirement. PHANTOM_CLOUD_TOKEN is not a supported Cloud bearer-token input, and the local vault passphrase is not the separate Cloud encryption key. Adding those GitHub secrets does not enable unattended Cloud pull.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scaffold-the-workflow">Check workflow availability</h2>

  <Code
    code={`# Inspect the installed command interface
stack ci init --help

# Reviewed source refuses generation with exit 1
stack ci init

# Compatibility flag: still refuses; does not overwrite
stack ci init --force`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    The source first checks whether <code>.stack.toml</code> exists; this is not TOML validation. If <code>.github/workflows/stack-ci.yml</code> already exists and <code>--force</code> is absent, it reports that the file was not changed. Otherwise it reports generation unavailable. All these command-body refusal paths exit 1 before any workflow directory creation or file write. <code>--force</code> only changes which refusal is reached; it is not an overwrite override.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-generated-workflow">Refusal output</h2>

  <Code
    code={`CI health workflow unavailable: Phantom Cloud pull requires trusted-terminal approval. No workflow was written.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h2 id="repository-secrets">Cloud prerequisites</h2>
  <p>
    These names appeared in the former Cloud recipe. Adding them in <strong>Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions</strong> does not enable unattended Cloud pull with Phantom Secrets 0.7.9:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 32%">Secret</th>
        <th>Where it comes from</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>PHANTOM_VAULT_PASSPHRASE</code></td>
        <td>
          A local-vault passphrase is not Phantom Secrets 0.7.9’s separate Cloud encryption key. It does not enable unattended Cloud pull.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>PHANTOM_CLOUD_TOKEN</code></td>
        <td>
          Phantom Secrets 0.7.9 reads its Cloud bearer token from its credential store, not this environment variable. Check <code>phantom --help</code>; this environment variable is not supported Cloud authentication.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Exit-code behaviour</h2>

  <Code
    code={`# Illustrative source refusal; no workflow write
$ stack ci init
CI health workflow unavailable: Phantom Cloud pull requires trusted-terminal approval. No workflow was written.
$ echo $?
1`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    The existing config and file guards also refuse with exit 1. Running a GitHub job manually does not supply the trusted-terminal ceremony required by Phantom Secrets 0.7.9. The producer does not emit a Cloud workflow, and no unattended health integration is established here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="json-report-shape">Optional source-drift report</h2>

  <Code
    code={`{
  "configured": ["supabase"],
  "detected": [],
  "orphans": ["supabase"],
  "untracked": []
}`}
    lang="json"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    The JSON above illustrates <code>stack doctor --reconcile --json</code>: <code>configured</code> and{" "}
    <code>detected</code> record config services and source detections; orphans and untracked record their differences. This branch returns before Phantom and health checks and returns 0 on detected drift. Tooling must inspect the fields; scanner or I/O errors can still fail. It is not credential or provider-health acceptance.
  </p>

  <h2>Other CI systems</h2>
  <p>
    <code>stack ci init</code> does not generate a health workflow for GitHub Actions or another CI system. An optional source-drift report can be collected separately:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`# Optional local source-drift report; drift itself returns 0
stack doctor --reconcile --json > stack-source-drift.json`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    A supported unattended credentialed health integration remains separate work. Open
    an issue on{" "}
    <a
      href="https://github.com/ashlrai/ashlr-stack/issues"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      target="_blank"
    >GitHub</a> if you want yours prioritised.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scan-in-ci"><code>stack scan</code> in CI — existing repos</h2>
  <p>
    If you're adopting Stack in a repo that already has services wired up
    manually (environment variables set, packages installed), use{" "}
    <code>stack scan</code> to detect them without interactive prompts:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`# Report detected services without adding them
stack scan --json --confidence medium`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 28%">Flag</th>
        <th>Behaviour</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--json</code></td>
        <td>Emit a machine-readable JSON report and exit 0. No prompts, and nothing is added even with <code>--yes</code>. Suitable for parsing in downstream steps.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--yes</code></td>
        <td>Without <code>--json</code>, implies <code>--auto</code> and enables add attempts at or above the confidence tier without confirmation. With <code>--json</code>, scan reports hits and returns before adding services. Without either flag, a non-TTY run prints a hint.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--confidence &lt;low|medium|high&gt;</code></td>
        <td>Filter by the named tier. The default is medium, or high with <code>--yes</code>; the example explicitly selects medium.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Illustrative JSON output shape:</p>

  <Code
    code={`{
  "hits": [
    { "provider": "resend", "confidence": "high", "sources": ["package.json:resend"] },
    { "provider": "supabase", "confidence": "high", "sources": ["package.json:@supabase/supabase-js"] }
  ]
}`}
    lang="json"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Combine with <code>stack doctor --reconcile</code> to also detect drift
    between <code>.stack.toml</code> and source imports. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/maintenance/">Maintenance</a> for details.
  </p>

  <h2>Rotating secrets from CI</h2>
  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <strong>Rotate at the provider using its supported process.</strong> Stack doctor reports available checks; it does not rotate credentials. <code>stack login &lt;provider&gt;</code> runs the provider login path, but cached credentials and create-only storage can prevent replacement. Verify the resulting slot and your separately qualified CI credential delivery before relying on it.
  </div>
</Docs>
